  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Logic of Experimentation has already shown clear early scholarly and usage impact: it has been downloaded 5,095 times and cited 5 times, indicating that the book is being read and taken up within musicology and performance-related research. The record also shows support from the European Research Council and open-access availability, which likely helped widen access.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2018, Paulo de Assis\u2019s Logic of Experimentation: Rethinking Music Performance Through Artistic Research has attracted a modest but visible scholarly response alongside substantial readership. The citation record is still early, but it is not isolated: citing works appear across 2019, 2020, 2021, and 2025, suggesting that the book\u2019s arguments have continued to circulate after publication rather than peaking in a single moment.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The shape of the citing landscape points to disciplinary relevance in musicology, performance studies, and adjacent arts research. Most citing works are journal articles, with one later book among them, and the venues include Revista V\u00f3rtex, twentieth-century music, Dramaturgias, and Quodlibet. This pattern suggests that the book is being used as a conceptual reference in discussions of musical performance, artistic research, aesthetics, and historiography.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The download record is especially strong for an academic monograph. More than 5,000 downloads through OAPEN indicate sustained interest from readers, complementing the citation signal and suggesting the book is being consulted beyond the small set of works that have formally cited it so far. Its open-access status likely supports this wider reach.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Additional signals reinforce its academic positioning. The book is held in library collections under subjects including theory of music and musicology, music philosophy and aesthetics, and performance, which is consistent with its interdisciplinary framing. The record also notes funding associated with the European Research Council. Overall, the evidence shows a book with active use and emerging scholarly uptake, especially within research communities concerned with experimental performance and artistic research.</p>",
